{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788311647,"nonce":"/89dG7lcOplX7CSaWo8VmFb0JCU=","title":"**Filed mid-run (m1-20260901, tick ~47,365), Evan's call.** Evan, watching the window: *\"you didn't check to see if you could build the heater or not… this is a deterministic thing, heater takes 5, pawns are at 4, the game already knows you can't build it, just have to pass that on.\"* He raised Lacey's Construction to 8 by hand so the run could continue — recorded as a human intervention in the run report.","message":"The barracks layout `ly-1` was preflighted with `place-layout --dry-run`, which reported a material shortfall of exactly 6 steel, was corrected, re-run clean (`shortfall: []`), and placed. 33 elements, zero blockers, zero refusals.\n\n**32 of 33 built.** The 33rd is the `Heater`, and `construction {layout_id:\"ly-1\"}` reports it as:\n\n```\n{\"def\":\"Heater\",\"at\":[111,137],\"state\":\"awaiting-materials\",\n \"worker\":null,\"missing\":[{\"def\":\"ComponentIndustrial\",\"count\":1}],\"blocking\":null}\n```\n\nThat diagnosis is wrong in a way that costs real time. There are **30 unforbidden `ComponentIndustrial` on the map, reachable** — the same dry-run that priced this layout reported `{\"def\":\"ComponentIndustrial\",\"count\":1,\"available\":30}`. Nothing is missing. The blueprint is unbuildable because `Heater.constructionSkillPrerequisite` is **5** and the best Construction on the roster was **4** (Lacey 4, Jimmy 4, Wouter 1). No pawn can take the job, so `WorkGiver_ConstructDeliverResources` never hauls the component, so the element reports as short of a material it is not short of.\n\n\n`RimWorld/GenConstruct.CanConstruct`, line 167 of the 1.6 decompile:\n\n```csharp\nif (checkSkills) {\n    if (p.skills != null) {\n        if (p.skills.GetSkill(SkillDefOf.Construction).Level \u003c t.def.constructionSkillPrerequisite) {\n            JobFailReason.Is(SkillTooLowTrans.Formatted(SkillDefOf.Construction.LabelCap));\n            return false;\n        }\n        if (p.skills.GetSkill(SkillDefOf.Artistic).Level \u003c t.def.artisticSkillPrerequisite) {\n            JobFailReason.Is(SkillTooLowTrans.Formatted(SkillDefOf.Artistic.LabelCap));\n            return false;\n        }\n    }\n    ...\n}\n```\n\n`BuildableDef.constructionSkillPrerequisite` and `artisticSkillPrerequisite` are plain ints on the def. The roster's levels are a plain read. The comparison needs no simulation, no pathfinding and no tick — it is decidable at preflight, which is exactly the standard `place-layout` already holds itself to for materials and per-cell blockers.\n\nThe game itself surfaces it twice: `ThingDef.SpecialDisplayStats` yields a `SkillRequiredToBuild` stat entry (`ThingDef.cs:2045`), and `Designator_Build` consults it. Vanilla's own siege AI reads it directly (`LordToil_Siege.cs:231` takes `Mathf.Max` of Sandbags' and Mortar's prerequisites before choosing what to build). So this is not obscure — it is a published, load-bearing field that our preflight does not look at.\n\n\n`rwa/README.md` §place-layout teaches the `unresolved_items` triage as:\n\n\u003e `awaiting-materials` on all of them is an unreachable-material colony, `blocked` is something in the way, `ready` with nobody working is a work-priority problem.\n\nThere is no fourth branch, and the skill ceiling lands in the first one wearing the wrong costume. Following that table, the correct next move looks like \"unforbid more components\" or \"check Hauling priorities\" — both of which are already fine, and neither of which will ever finish the heater. The run would have burned the diagnosis loop had Evan not been watching the screen.\n\n\nAcross all seven of the run's authored layouts, exactly two defs are gated at a roster max of 4:\n\n| def | constructionSkillPrerequisite | in |\n|---|---|---|\n| `Heater` | **5** | barracks |\n| `Cooler` | **5** | freezer |\n| `WoodFiredGenerator` | 4 | power room — met exactly, no margin |\n\nEvery other def in the corpus (Wall, Door, Bed, FueledStove, SimpleResearchBench, TableSculpting, TableStonecutter, HandTailoringBench, Table2x2c, Stool, Shelf, TorchLamp, HorseshoesPin, HoopstoneRing, GameOfUrBoard, ChessTable) is 0. So the blast radius is small and the consequence is not: those two defs are the *entire* electric-heat and freezer plan, which is what `barracks-heat` (daily.md) and T1(d)'s `food_days \u003e= 6` both rest on. A silent skill ceiling on `Cooler` would have surfaced as rotted food on day 20 with no other symptom.\n\n\n1. `place-layout --dry-run` and `build --dry-run` publish a skill verdict per element: the def's `constructionSkillPrerequisite` / `artisticSkillPrerequisite`, the best level on the roster, the pawns that clear it, and a named shortfall when nobody does. Shape it like `shortfall[]` — a measurement plus a `hint` that names the fix (\"nobody has Construction 5; the best is Lacey at 4\"), since that is the pattern that worked for the steel shortfall in this very run.\n2. A non-dry-run `place-layout` that contains an element no colonist can build **says so on the placement result**, not only on a later `construction` read. Whether it refuses the transaction (consistent with \"preflight every cell first; on any failure place NOTHING\") or places and warns is the design question — the material shortfall precedent argues for refusing without `--partial`.\n3. `construction` distinguishes the two causes. `awaiting-materials` must not be returned for an element whose materials are available and whose blocker is the skill gate; add a state or a reason field so `unresolved_items` triage has a fourth branch. The `worker: null` field is the natural place to carry *why* there is no worker.\n4. `rwa/README.md`'s `unresolved_items` triage table gains that branch, and `checklists/triggered.md` gains an act-keyed line after any `place-layout` / `build`: confirm somebody can actually build every element. A lesson no checklist cites is half-landed.\n5. `accept/\u003cid\u003e-skill-prereq.py` on a bench: place a `Heater` with a roster capped below 5, assert the dry-run names the skill shortfall, assert the non-dry-run behaves per (2), and assert `construction` does not call it a material problem. Then raise the skill and assert it builds.\n\n\n- `artisticSkillPrerequisite` has the identical shape and no coverage. The run's sculpture line (T2 wealth, Jimmy at Artistic 6) depends on it.\n- `TerrainDef` carries `constructionSkillPrerequisite` too (`TerrainTemplateDef.cs:28`, `TerrainDefGenerator_Carpet.cs`), so floors are gated by the same field and the IR's `terrain` layer will hit it.\n- `p.IsColonyMech` takes the `RaceProps.mechFixedSkillLevel` branch instead (GenConstruct.cs:178-190). Out of scope for M1, but any implementation that reads only `p.skills` will be wrong for mechs.","files":null}]}